# PART 3 — GOOGLE DRIVE DUAL BOOK LIBRARY

This is one of the most important permanent requirements.

The platform must support two independent book libraries.

## 3.1 Shared Book Library

This library is controlled by the Application Owner.

Requirements:

- The Owner can upload books directly from the application.
- Uploaded files must be saved in the Owner's Google Drive.
- The application must maintain the relationship between each book and its Google Drive file.
- The Owner can organize books by subject, category, examination, chapter, and other useful metadata.
- The Owner can grant individual users access to specific books.
- The Owner can revoke access whenever necessary.
- The Owner can delete books.
- The Owner can inspect book metadata, upload history, file size, and processing status.

Members must not receive direct Google Drive access.

## 3.2 Personal Book Library

Each registered user must have a separate Personal Book Library.

Requirements:

- Users can upload their personal books through an in-app Upload Portal.
- They must not need Google Drive accounts or Drive permissions.
- Files must be stored in a separate Personal Book Storage area in the Owner's Google Drive.
- Each user's files must be logically isolated using their authenticated internal user identity.
- A user can access their own books.
- Other users cannot access these books automatically.
- The Application Owner can view and delete any user's uploaded book.
- The Owner can grant selected users access to a particular Personal Book.
- The Owner can revoke such access at any time.

The application must not provide direct Google Drive folder links to ordinary members.

## 3.3 Personal Book Sharing

The Owner must be able to share a user's Personal Book with one or more selected users.

Example:

Member A uploads a Mathematics book.

The Owner grants Member B and Member C permission to use it.

Then:

- Member B can read the book and create personal notes, questions, exams, and progress records.
- Member C can independently use the same book.
- Member B cannot access Member C's notes or progress.
- Member C cannot access Member B's notes or progress.
- Neither member can change the original book's ownership or sharing permissions.
- Only the Owner controls book-sharing permissions.

A shared Personal Book must remain associated with its original uploader and owner.

## 3.4 Google Drive Storage Architecture

Recommended logical structure:

Google Drive
└── Job Preparation AI
    ├── Shared Book Library
    │   ├── Bangla
    │   ├── English
    │   ├── Mathematics
    │   ├── General Knowledge
    │   └── Other Subjects
    │
    └── Personal Book Library
        ├── User A
        ├── User B
        └── User C

The actual storage implementation must be designed around secure Google Drive API access.

Requirements:

- Google Drive API v3.
- Google Picker for selecting existing files where appropriate.
- OAuth 2.0.
- Evaluate the least-privilege `drive.file` scope first.
- Do not request broad Drive access unless technically necessary and explicitly approved.
- Explain OAuth verification and restricted-scope implications where applicable.
- Store sensitive OAuth credentials securely on the server.
- Encrypt refresh tokens at rest.
- Never expose OAuth tokens or private Drive file IDs unnecessarily to clients.
- Use server-side Drive operations.
- Handle token expiration, revocation, file deletion, file movement, and account disconnection.

Do not make files public.

Do not create permanent public download links.

