# PART 6 — USER ROLES AND PERMISSION ARCHITECTURE

Implement a server-enforced role and permission architecture.

Required roles:

1. Owner
2. Admin
3. Member

The Owner must have ultimate control over:

- User management.
- Shared Book Library.
- Personal Book Library.
- Book permissions.
- Feature permissions.
- Google Drive connection.
- Upload restrictions.
- Book deletion.
- Access logs.

Admin permissions must be explicitly delegated by the Owner.

Admins must not independently control the Owner's Google Drive credentials or delete the Owner's original files without explicit authorization.

Members can use only the books and features permitted to them.

## 6.1 Per-Book Permissions

The Owner must be able to configure permissions separately for every user and every book.

Required permissions:

- View Book
- AI Reader
- PDF/Text Extraction
- Generate Questions
- Take Exam
- AI Tutor
- Notes
- Revision
- Download Original

The Download Original permission must remain permanently disabled under the current project policy.

The Owner must be able to:

- Grant permissions.
- Revoke permissions.
- Update permissions.
- Set optional expiration dates.
- View access history.

## 6.2 Backend Authorization

Every protected API request must verify:

1. Firebase authentication.
2. User account status.
3. Book ownership or authorized access.
4. Per-book permission.
5. Feature permission.
6. Google Drive connection and file status.
7. Permission expiration or revocation.

Never trust user IDs, book IDs, file IDs, or permission claims supplied by the frontend without server-side validation.

Do not rely only on hiding frontend buttons.

Every AI API route must enforce the same authorization rules.

## 6.3 Permission Revocation

When access is revoked:

- New requests must be denied immediately.
- Cached permissions must be invalidated or revalidated.
- Existing processing jobs must not continue exposing restricted content.
- User access logs must record the relevant event.

Deleting a book or revoking access must not falsely imply that previously downloaded, copied, or screenshot content can be remotely erased.

